UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 24, Lesson 1: Artists and their work', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Artists make choices about subject, colour, line and materials, and these choices create a style. Piet Mondrian, a Dutch painter, made paintings using straight black lines in a grid with blocks of red, yellow, blue and white. Henri Matisse, a French artist, made bold paper cut-outs late in his life by cutting painted paper into shapes. When you study an artist, look for their technique, which is how they made the work, and the effect it has. Name the artist, title, materials and an element they used, and give your own response with a reason.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCompare a grid painting made of straight black lines with a paper cut-out of curvy shapes. Name one technique each and its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''The grid uses straight lines and primary colours, so it feels ordered and calm. The cut-out uses curvy cut shapes and bright colours, so it feels playful.'' Each answer names the technique and the effect.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that artists all make art the same way. Every artist makes different choices, and looking closely at their technique helps us see how they made their work and why it looks that way.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose two artists from a library book or a gallery website with an adult. For each, write the name, an artwork title, one technique and one element. Compare them in three sentences and give a reason for your favourite. Do not copy the whole artwork.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Artists make choices about subject, colour, line and materials, and these choices create a style. Piet Mondrian, a Dutch painter, made paintings using straight black lines in a grid with blocks of red, yellow, blue and white. Henri Matisse, a French artist, made bold paper cut-outs late in his life by cutting painted paper into shapes. When you study an artist, look for their technique, which is how they made the work, and the effect it has. Name the artist, title, materials and an element they used, and give your own response with a reason.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose two artists from a library book or a gallery website with an adult. For each, write the name, an artwork title, one technique and one element. Compare them in three sentences and give a reason for your favourite.  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using an artist on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking that artists all make art the same way.. Correction: Strong answer: ''The grid uses straight lines and primary colours, so it feels ordered and calm.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that artists all make art the same way. Every artist makes different choices, and looking closely at their technique helps us see how they made their work and why it looks that way. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating an artist on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with an artist, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about an artist with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for an artist. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for an artist. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''The grid uses straight lines and primary colours, so it feels ordered and calm.","Thinking that artists all make art the same way."],"question":"Compare a grid painting made of straight black lines with a paper cut-out of curvy shapes. Name one technique each and its effect. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for an artist; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that artists all make art the same way.","Artists make choices about subject, colour, line and materials, and these choices create a style.","''The grid uses straight lines and primary colours, so it feels ordered and calm.","Careful use of an artwork and explain why it matters with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with an artist?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named an artwork and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that artists all make art the same way.","Name an artwork and explain why it matters only, without linking it to an artist.","Apply an artist to new material and name the an artwork and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"Which response best applies an artist with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on an artist. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that artists all make art the same way.","''The grid uses straight lines and primary colours, so it feels ordered and calm.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for an artist?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that artists all make art the same way.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for an artist."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about an artist?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: Looking at a Mondrian-style grid, Pia says: ''The artist used thick black lines and large blocks of red, blue","Thinking that artists all make art the same way.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Artists make choices about subject, colour, line and materials, and these choices create a style. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for an artist?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name an artwork and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Choose two artists from a library book or a gallery website with an adult.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Thinking that artists all make art the same way."],"question":"Which independent response best applies an artist to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about an artist with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating an artist on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using an artist on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for an artist. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with an artist, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking that artists all make art the same way.. Correction: Strong answer: ''The grid uses straight lines and primary colours, so it feels ordered and calm.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w24-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise an artist.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use an artist with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify an artwork and explain why it matters.","I can practise a style independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a technique needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Artists make choices about subject, colour, line and materials, and these choices create a style.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Artists make choices about subject, colour, line and materials, and these choices create a style.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about an artist with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Looking at a Mondrian-style grid, Pia says: ''The artist used thick black lines and large blocks of red, blue and yellow. The straight lines make it feel neat and balanced, and the red block draws my eye first.'' She names the technique and the effect. For Matisse she says: ''The cut-out shapes are curvy and bold and feel playful.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nCompare a grid painting made of straight black lines with a paper cut-out of curvy shapes. Name one technique each and its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''The grid uses straight lines and primary colours, so it feels ordered and calm. The cut-out uses curvy cut shapes and bright colours, so it feels playful.'' Each answer names the technique and the effect.","explain":"Artists make choices about subject, colour, line and materials, and these choices create a style. Piet Mondrian, a Dutch painter, made paintings using straight black lines in a grid with blocks of red, yellow, blue and white. Henri Matisse, a French artist, made bold paper cut-outs late in his life by cutting painted paper into shapes. When you study an artist, look for their technique, which is how they made the work, and the effect it has. Name the artist, title, materials and an element they used, and give your own response with a reason.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that artists all make art the same way. Every artist makes different choices, and looking closely at their technique helps us see how they made their work and why it looks that way. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two artists, two styles.","caption":"Technique shapes style.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Grid</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">straight black lines</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cut-out</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">curvy bold shapes</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Looking at a Mondrian-style grid, Pia says: ''The artist used thick black lines and large blocks of red, blue an Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Looking at a Mondrian-style grid, Pia says: ''The artist used thick black lines and large blocks of red, blue an Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about an artist with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Looking at a Mondrian-style grid, Pia says: ''The artist used thick black lines and large blocks of red, blue and yellow. The straight lines make it feel neat and balanced, and the red block draws my eye first.'' She names the technique and the effect. For Matisse she says: ''The cut-out shapes are curvy and bold and feel playful.'' Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Artist details and effect.","caption":"Name the effect.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Name</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">artist, title, material</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Effect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">what it does for the viewer</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about an artist with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that artists all make art the same way. Every artist makes different choices, and looking closely at their technique helps us see how they made their work and why it looks that way. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Looking at a Mondrian-style grid, Pia says: ''The artist used thick black lines and large blocks of red, blue and yellow. The straight lines make it feel neat and balanced, and the red block draws my eye first.'' She names the technique and the effect. For Matisse she says: ''The cut-out shapes are curvy and bold and feel playful.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''The grid uses straight lines and primary colours, so it feels ordered and calm. The cut-out uses curvy cut shapes and bright colours, so it feels playful.'' Each answer names the technique and the effect.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that artists all make art the same way. Every artist makes different choices, and looking closely at their technique helps us see how they made their work and why it looks that way. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about an artist with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nArtists make choices about subject, colour, line and materials, and these choices create a style. Piet Mondrian, a Dutch painter, made paintings using straight black lines in a grid with blocks of red, yellow, blue and white. Henri Matisse, a French artist, made bold paper cut-outs late in his life by cutting painted paper into shapes. When you study an artist, look for their technique, which is how they made the work, and the effect it has. Name the artist, title, materials and an element they used, and give your own response with a reason. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 24, Lesson 1: Artists and their work","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Printmaking, collage and texture","week":24,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Looking at a Mondrian-style grid, Pia says: ''The artist used thick black lines and large blocks of red, blue and yellow. The straight lines make it feel neat and balanced, and the red block draws my eye first.'' She names the technique and the effect. For Matisse she says: ''The cut-out shapes are curvy and bold and feel playful.''\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two artists, two styles.","caption":"Technique shapes style.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Grid</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">straight black lines</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cut-out</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">curvy bold shapes</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Artist details and effect.","caption":"Name the effect.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Name</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">artist, title, material</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Effect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">what it does for the viewer</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w24-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
